# 18. Content-Length / Repetition Rules

These are deliberately conservative.

## CP-LENGTH-001 — Empty Sections
**Severity:** HARD

A required structural section must not be empty.

## CP-LENGTH-002 — Suspiciously Short Practical Section
**Severity:** WARNING

A hands-on section that contains no meaningful task/step content should be flagged.

Do not set an arbitrary word-count threshold as a quality gate.

## CP-LENGTH-003 — Repetition
**Severity:** NOT YET

Repeated or padded content is a real quality concern, but deterministic repetition detection should not be part of the first QA CLI.

Later implementation may use semantic similarity or AI review.

---

# 19. Safety and Security Rules for Content

## CP-SAFE-001 — No Secrets
**Severity:** HARD

Content should not contain obvious real secrets, API keys, private keys, passwords, tokens, or credential material.

This should eventually include deterministic secret-pattern scanning.

## CP-SAFE-002 — Lab Scope
**Severity:** HUMAN

Security labs must be framed for authorised environments.

The first deterministic QA release should not attempt to judge whether every command is safe.

## CP-SAFE-003 — Dangerous Automation
**Severity:** HUMAN / later specialist review

Do not automatically execute arbitrary security commands from content.

Any future technical-validation agent must use explicitly approved, isolated environments.

---

# 20. What the First QA CLI Should NOT Attempt

The first implementation must not pretend to solve these:

1. Full technical accuracy
2. Full real-world incident verification
3. Whether a lab is pedagogically excellent
4. Whether an artifact is genuinely portfolio-worthy
5. Whether the writing sounds human
6. Whether a module is repetitive in a meaningful semantic sense
7. Whether a tool is the best enterprise choice
8. Whether a salary figure is current
9. Whether a job-market claim is current
10. Whether a learner can successfully complete a lab in every environment
11. Whether a real-world scenario is legally/ethically complete
12. Whether a resource is authoritative merely because its URL resolves

Those belong in later AI-assisted or human review stages.

